<WikiLayout title="Domains | Underlords Wiki" description="The rooms, territories, recurring locations, and named spaces of Underlords.">
  <section class="hero"><div><div class="kicker">DOMAINS · ROOMS · TERRITORIES</div><h1>Domains</h1><p class="tagline">The place names are part of the mythology: HQ, the Wall, the Casino, the Panic Room, the Headache Room, the couch, the garden, and several locations created because one recurring behavior apparently deserved zoning.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="index-list">
    {locations.map((location)=><article class="index-card" id={location.id}><small>{location.type}</small><h3>{location.name}</h3><p>{location.logline}</p></article>)}
    <article class="index-card" id="wall-orientation-speedrun"><small>Wall grammar · October 19, 2020</small><h3>The Wall Explains Itself in Eight and a Half Minutes</h3><p>Plushie asks what the channel is for. Rooks gives the operating definition: <code>When we catch people say dumb stuff uwu</code>. Plushie answers <code>Okie doki</code>. Eight minutes later Rooks warns the room to move the chatter to Whiskey before Snow comes after everybody, and Plushie immediately produces <code>Snow u saw nothing UwU</code>. Question to concealment attempt: about eight and a half minutes.</p><p>This is not Plushie's first Wall appearance, and whatever Snow was supposedly not meant to see is not recoverable from the bounded exchange. The useful part is the speed of the social lesson. Rooks explains the room as a place where dumb things get caught; minutes later Plushie is already joking in the room's evidence-destruction dialect. Orientation complete. Nobody even handed out a pamphlet.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="wall-production-pipeline"><small>Receipt engineering · 2020–2023</small><h3>The Wall Had a Production Pipeline</h3><p>By September 2020, public disgrace already had tooling. Ren posts a receipt and Anthos immediately asks <code>how r u so quick!!??</code>. Ren explains: <code>My iPad has a quick editing option</code>, then gives the whole workflow in one cursed sentence: <code>So I take it post it and delete UwU</code>. Anthos contrasts that with having to use Photoshop on a laptop; Hishiro later points out that Android can screenshot and edit immediately too. The Wall was not just where screenshots ended up. People were thinking about capture, crop, filing speed, and what happened to the local copy afterward.</p><p>A year later Tofu supplies the failure mode. During a multi-image filing burst they admit <code>I was lurking and then I messed up bu@posting in whiskey</code>, then eventually confess <code>I posted it@so@quick I didn’t crop it wells</code>. That is the technical appendix to the first-to-file joke: justice was moving fast enough that crop quality became collateral damage. Whiskey is a named source/trigger venue in Tofu's explanation, but no exact nearby Wall attachment is hard-joined to a Whiskey source file yet.</p><p>Then in September 2023 Ren finally gives provenance the sentence it had been begging for: <code>But I also took a SS of the yell</code>. Twenty-one seconds later Ren posts <code>IMG_7950.png</code> with <code>Use it irresponsibly ❤️</code>. The personal screenshot capture is confirmed; the same-author timing makes that exact file the screenshot Ren just described <strong>probable/strong</strong>, not hard, because there is still no reply pointer, hash, or pixel inspection joining the two. The caption is almost the Wall's entire distribution philosophy in four words and a heart: acquire evidence, hand it to the room, and apparently waive all responsibility for whatever these people do next. Ren is confirmed as poster and confirmed as having personally taken a screenshot in the scene. MADE BY, FEATURING, the exact image contents, and actual downstream reuse remain unresolved. The pipeline is real; the attribution jobs still refuse to become soup.</p><p>The room eventually exposes the opposite end of that pipeline too: what happens when the embarrassing thing happens <em>inside the Wall itself</em>. On August 31, 2023 Torr explains that “they” means the seeds are the babies; a still-unresolved deleted account realizes they misunderstood, apologizes, and Torr dryly points out that nobody now has to <code>take a screenshot and post it in wall of shame</code>. The joke only works because the normal lifecycle is already second nature: embarrassing line somewhere else → somebody takes a receipt → somebody files it here. If you embarrass yourself while already standing in the evidence locker, the Wall has achieved zero-copy prosecution. No repost needed. The deleted account stays unresolved, and the surrounding image remains only POSTED BY Xuseio; its maker, capturer, and subjects are not invented from the joke.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="wall-receipt-appeals"><small>Receipt appeals · 2020–2022</small><h3>The Receipts Developed an Appeals Process</h3><p>The first surviving Wall night already has editorial taste. Ren labels one filing <code>Personal favorite</code>; less than six minutes later another gets <code>This one is saved as favorite</code>. Two years later Mugen is still posting <code>My favorites tonight</code> and <code>Tonight's personal favorite</code>. The Wall was not merely storing evidence. People were ranking the evidence. Some crimes apparently arrived pre-nominated for Best Picture.</p><p>Then preservation starts beating deletion. In October 2022 Torr complains that Tae is the <code>trouble maker</code> because Torr tried to delete a <code>completely ambiguous comment</code> and Tae saved it. Torr's defense immediately collapses into repeating <code>totally ambiguous</code> and trying to recruit Rummy as a friendly ruling. A nearby Tae-posted image is the strong probable saved receipt, but the exact file join is not hard without a reply/hash/pixel edge. The social mechanism is confirmed anyway: deleting your own line did not necessarily end the case if somebody else had already decided it deserved a future.</p><p>March 2022 shows that a screenshot was not automatically sacred scripture either. Tae looks at one of Ren's filings and asks <code>wheres the context</code>; Ren answers a nearby <code>what is this</code> with the gloriously useless <code>A screen shoot</code>. Four days later Marian/Panda files Eos, Eos true-replies that there is context, Marian says <code>do share</code>, and Eos answers with a second attachment literally named <code>prooooooof.png</code>. The Wall's remedy for incomplete evidence was, naturally, more evidence. Whether Exhibit B actually exonerated anybody is not preserved.</p><p>By October 2021 the receipts themselves could be put on trial. ShiyaX posts an <code>image0.png</code> with <code>Believing is Seeing 🤔 ?</code>. A pooled Deleted User counterfiles a different <code>image0.png</code> and says Shiya edited the first state; Shiya true-replies to that exact competing exhibit with <code>Your’s say (edited)</code>. Nobody here gets declared correct: the pixels were not adjudicated, the pooled account remains pooled, and the files are not assumed to depict the same underlying message. What is confirmed is much funnier. The Wall had reached the stage where screenshot evidence could summon counterevidence, version arguments, context motions, and appeals. The evidence locker had developed case law by accident.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="wall-self-prosecution"><small>Courtroom grammar · 2020–2021</small><h3>The Wall Invents Self-Prosecution</h3><p>Snow found the procedural loophole first — or at least the earliest surviving direct version of it currently recovered. On October 30, 2020, after Zoshaa/NintendoShitcube posts <code>image0.png</code>, adds <code>O w o</code>, and Ren starts laughing, Snow unveils his defense: <code>you cant wall of shame me in the wall of shame room if I shamed myself first 😛</code>. The tongue-out face is doing legal work here. Snow is not expressing remorse; he is arguing that pre-owning the embarrassment voids everybody else's jurisdiction. Unfortunately, Zoshaa appears to have already brought paperwork. Zoshaa is POSTED BY for the adjacent image; its exact relation to Snow is probable/strong from the exchange, while MADE BY, CAPTURED BY, FEATURING, and the unseen pixels remain unresolved.</p><p>Six days later the room already sounds like it has a jury. Anthos posts a screenshot; Ren opens with <code>In my defense</code> and immediately abandons counsel: <code>I got no defense, jury send me to jail</code>. Anthos answers <code>u said it was shame worthy i am just obliging</code>. Euros/Illien says <code>I was coming to do the same</code>, then supplies jail imagery and <code>Noodle in jail</code>. The affectionate follow-up matters: Ren goes right back to <code>I luv Chu</code>, Anthos answers <code>mwaah~</code>. This is not adversarial discipline wearing a funny hat. It is consensual clown court: defendant concedes the material, prosecutor claims civic duty, the gallery provides sentencing graphics, everybody kisses before imaginary transport. Anthos is POSTED BY for the screenshot; its visual subject remains unresolved, and <code>Noodle</code> stays scene wording rather than becoming a canonical Ren alias on one filing.</p><p>Self-shame then develops separate use cases instead of one universal rule. On May 26, 2021 Marian/Panda posts <code>eos.PNG</code> and declares <code>even if i have to shame myself while doing it, i'm getting eos on this wall</code>: collateral self-incrimination is apparently acceptable if Eos also goes down. In August, Vanness takes the cleaner route by filing themself and asking <code>Shame me</code>. So the surviving spectrum is beautifully stupid: Snow tries self-shame as immunity; Marian accepts it as prosecution costs; Vanness uses it as voluntary surrender. Marian is POSTED BY for <code>eos.PNG</code> and explicitly names Eos as the target, but unavailable pixels mean nobody is promoted to visual FEATURING, maker, or capturer by assumption.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="lobby-party-flyer"><small>Lobby · November–December 2020</small><h3>The Bouncer Also Hands You the Party Flyer</h3><p>Lobby was border control run by <strong>Underlords Stripclub Bouncer</strong>, but the humans kept turning admission into a live handoff instead of a dead permissions screen. On December 10 radiance finishes the IGN / club / age check and Mugen immediately tells them Mafia is happening right now: <code>come join if you'd like! ❤️</code>. Two days later Shayduh gets the role, the server map, and <code>Christmas movie marathon streaming now</code> in the theatre. KiuLiu gets the same movie-marathon routing later that day. The front door could end with “you are allowed in” and then, without changing breath, become “also everybody is over there, go say hi.”</p><p>The gatekeeping joke had continuity too. Drink Water Reminder uses the same <code>YOU SHALL NOT PASS</code> Gandalf Tenor during radiance's intake that had already appeared during Olenka's November 27 intake. Two separated newcomers are enough to stop treating it as one accidental GIF. DWR was apparently willing to translate access-control procedure into Middle-earth until people coughed up the form. Radiance answers with a naughty emote and then supplies <code>radiance / underoath / not minor</code>; the rule survives the joke just fine.</p><p>The invitation after intake does <em>not</em> prove Mafia or the movie marathon was why any particular person arrived. What it does show is a repeated onboarding instinct: do the paperwork, point at the map, then tell the newcomer where the house is currently making noise.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="staff-vacation"><small>Club-Only · December 22, 2020</small><h3>Real Life Filed for Leave and Won</h3><p>Ten days after checking in as <code>Shayduh / Underlords / 24</code>, Shayduh comes into Club-Only apologizing because life has been chaotic and asks who they are supposed to message if they will be gone for a while. The answer is much more useful than the role name ever was. braindead true-replies: <code>if u message someone with a higher role they can put you in staff vacation :) life will always be more important than gaming so dw</code>. Anthos follows on the same message: <code>irl is always first priority!</code> and tells Shayduh to come back rested and happy when ready.</p><p>So <strong>STAFF VACATION</strong> was not merely a weird fossil in the role list. People described an actual procedure: tell someone higher in the hierarchy that you expect to be away, and they could put you “in staff vacation.” The likely practical purpose was to preserve or hold status during a known absence. That also makes Mugen's later line about having <code>saved your account on vacation uwu</code> look very much like the same broader mechanism without proving LilithEl's exact role assignment.</p><p>The important part is the emotional order of operations. Shayduh arrives sounding guilty for disappearing; both replies solve the logistics while removing the guilt. Whatever the exact Discord-role / Dragon Raja-roster mechanics were, the stated policy was not “attendance matters.” It was literally <strong>real life first</strong>. Vacation and Titania's later <code>applied back</code> probably represent different return paths — one where absence was known and held, one where somebody had to reapply — but eligibility, the five-month question, and the restoration mechanics are still open. Also, despite the name, nothing here proves STAFF VACATION was limited to people holding the Staff role. UL naming has committed worse crimes.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="members-only-lounge"><small>Members-only voice room · April 2020</small><h3>☠ Members Only Lounge</h3><p>The secret room did not lose its text transcript. It never had one: the surviving Discord export identifies <code>☠ Members Only Lounge</code> as a voice channel with zero text messages, and its channel ID dates the object to April 3, 2020 — almost three days before <code>💬｜club-only</code>. UL had already built somewhere private to sit around and talk before the later main text room existed.</p><p>By May 2024, a room called <code>Mugens Lounge</code> was mysterious enough that Rummy admitted she had never been in it, Ren said <code>no one but mugs know</code>, and then upgraded the unknown to <code>secret boss lair id say</code>. It was probably the old Members Only Lounge or a successor in the same private-room lineage, but the recovered 2024 exchange has no direct channel ID, so the exact bridge stays probable. Which somehow improves the joke: nobody can explain the room, therefore obviously the boss is doing classified boss shit in there.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="officers-challenge"><small>Challenge room · 2022–2023</small><h3>😈│ul-officers-challenge</h3><p>The title sounds like Officers were competing with each other behind a velvet rope. The surviving instructions say almost the opposite. On February 19, 2022 Ansun explicitly clarified that Officers Challenge was <code>for anyone to participate in</code> on the relevant challenge surface: different Officers supplied and hosted different prompts, while the audience could suggest future ones by DM or tag. A March reminder makes the asynchronous mechanic even clearer: <strong>pinned</strong> challenges were still open, so participants could come back and do them whenever convenient.</p><p>The attribution matters because this room had several jobs happening at once. On March 27 Ansun announces <code>NEW OFFICER CHALLENGE FROM @ƐℲı˥!!</code>; the announcement is Ansun's, while the challenge is explicitly from Sye/Life. The best read is less tournament bracket than standing dare shelf: Officers stocked it, people with access could browse the pinned crimes on their own time, and suggestions could feed the next batch. <code>Anyone</code> is not a claim that the whole internet had access, and a pinned challenge being open does not prove every old challenge stayed open forever.</p></article>
    <article class="index-card" id="daycare"><small>Younger-cast social room</small><h3>UL Daycare</h3><p>A comfort/play room whose surviving 2021–2022 use shows that social presence did not map neatly onto current in-game club labels.</p><p>By 2021, current club label clearly was not the whole social point of the room. Nori arrived explicitly calling Underlords his club, Damon introduced himself as Purgatory's leader, and AJ cheerfully admitted he was in <code>a random club lol</code>; all three still turned up in Daycare almost immediately after Lobby routing. The room reads less like a roster checkpoint than the couch people kept ending up on. This does not prove universal access or any specific Discord-permission rule.</p><p>It also had the domestic logic of a house where an Eli command could become a tiny kitchen disaster. On January 4, 2021 Lilly issues the action <code>burns down moki's kitchen</code>; Eli generates the much more elaborate butter-and-gasoline narration; Lilly then immediately adds <code>Sorry moki</code>. The bot wrote the theatrical prose. Lilly supplied the arson-shaped command and the instant apology. One scene is not a recurring Lilly–moki feud, but it is an excellent Daycare specimen: commit nonsense first, apologize to the homeowner second.</p></article>
  </div></section>
